/*
 * libz -- the jump table.
 *
 * ORDER IS THE ABI. An entry's POSITION in this file is the offset a
 * compiled program jumps to; nothing else identifies it. So:
 *
 *   ADD ONLY AT THE END. NEVER INSERT. NEVER REORDER. NEVER DELETE.
 *
 * Inserting one line moves every entry after it, and every binary
 * built against the old table then calls the wrong function --
 * silently, with plausible arguments, because the jump succeeds. That
 * is the failure this file exists to prevent, which is why
 * LIBZ_ABI_VERSION is checked before a single symbol is resolved
 * (docs/libz.md).
 *
 * -- Why this list is flat --
 *
 * It used to be grouped, with headings for memory, strings, the
 * filesystem and so on. That reads better and it fights the rule
 * above: a grouped list invites putting a new string function with
 * the other string functions, which is exactly the edit that breaks
 * every existing binary. The grouping was a comfort that made the
 * dangerous thing feel natural.
 *
 * Flat, with the index every sixteen lines, so that the only place a
 * new entry can go is obvious. Use `make` and read libz.sym if you
 * want to know what lives where; that file is generated and always
 * agrees with reality.
 *
 * Removing something is not allowed either. A function that is no
 * longer wanted keeps its slot -- see the RETIRED convention at the
 * end of this file.
 */

/* 0 */
